course: dictionary
lesson: 10080
title: Theft and what it means
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Theft is a noun, so it names a thing: the crime of stealing. Theft means taking something that belongs to someone else, without permission and without meaning to give it back. A person who steals is a thief.
explain: The word can help you work out a sentence. «"Shrinkage" is shop talk for goods lost to theft.» Goods are things a shop sells, and lost to theft means taken by stealing, so shrinkage means the shop has lost items to thieves. Check it by swapping in your meaning: "goods taken by stealing" still makes sense.
explain: Read the whole sentence before you decide. «Keeping something you know belongs to someone else can count as theft, even if you found it.» The words "even if you found it" are a clue: finding something does not make it yours. If you know whose it is and keep it, that can be theft.
fact: Theft is the crime of stealing: taking something that belongs to someone else without permission.
fact: A theft is one act of stealing, and a thief is the person who steals.
fact: «Putting a theft right» means giving back or paying back, and saying sorry to the person.
fact: «Identity theft» is when someone steals another person's personal details online, and «petty theft» means stealing small things.
fact: «From 1788 to 1868 Britain sent about 160,000 convicts on ships to Australia, a punishment called transportation, sometimes for quite small thefts.» This shows that a theft could be a small thing, and that the punishment was harsh.
exercise: What is theft? | Lending a book to a friend | Finding a lost toy and handing it to a teacher | Giving back something you borrowed | *The crime of taking something that belongs to someone else without permission
exercise: «Keeping something you know belongs to someone else can count as theft, even if you found it.» Can finding something be theft? | *Yes, if you know it belongs to someone else and you keep it | No, finding something is never theft | No, because a found thing is always yours | Yes, but only in a shop
exercise: Which word names a person who steals? | *thief | transportation | shrinkage | theft
test: «Identity theft» is when someone steals what? | Sweets from a corner shop | A bicycle left outside a school | A lost library book on the bus | *Another person's personal details online
test: What does putting a theft right mean? | Blaming a friend for it | *Giving or paying back what was taken, and saying sorry | Keeping quiet and hoping no one notices | Taking something else in return
exam: In «sometimes for quite small thefts», what does thefts mean? | *Acts of stealing | Large sums of money | Long journeys by ship | Punishments given by a judge
